PromQL Basics in Prometheus

🔹 What is PromQL? 🧠
 PromQL (Prometheus Query Language) is used to query and analyze metrics stored in Prometheus.
 It helps engineers search, filter, and visualize monitoring data in real-time 📊
👉 “How do we read monitoring data?”
 👉 The answer is: PromQL

🔹 Why it matters in real-world? 🏗️
 Without queries, metrics are just raw numbers.
 PromQL helps you:
✔ Analyze system performance
 ✔ Create dashboards 📈
 ✔ Build alerts 🚨
 ✔ Troubleshoot issues quickly

🔹 Basic Query Syntax: ⚡
A PromQL query usually starts with a metric name.
👉 Example:
cpu_usage

This returns CPU usage metrics.

🔹 Filtering in PromQL: 🔍
Filters help narrow down results using labels.
👉 Example:
cpu_usage{server="app1"}

✔ Shows CPU usage only for server app1

🔹 Basic Queries Examples: 📊
✔ CPU Usage
cpu_usage

✔ Memory Usage
memory_usage

✔ HTTP Requests
http_requests_total

✔ Filter by Instance
http_requests_total{instance="server1"}

🔹 Real-world Example: 💡
Your application becomes slow 🐢
👉 PromQL helps you check:
CPU usage
Request rate
Error count
👉 You quickly identify the issue 🚨

🔹 Key Concepts to Remember: 🧠
✔ PromQL = Query language 🧠
 ✔ Used to analyze metrics 📊
 ✔ Filters use labels 🔍
 ✔ Helps in dashboards & alerts 🚨
